{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Run inventory-api as a managed service on node1 listening on TCP port 8080.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The systemd unit is enabled and active, runs under the dedicated inventory-api account, listens on 0.0.0.0:8080, and returns valid health and inventory responses. The same service and listener recovered after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c1bfc2c04f6942f0","cmd-8d6a173620e748e4","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"Limit each process of the separate batchworker account to 128 MB of virtual memory.","status":"satisfied","summary":"PAM-created batchworker sessions directly reported matching soft and hard RLIMIT_AS values of 131072 KiB (134217728 bytes), both before and after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-875b5d12e00741a6","cmd-8d6a173620e748e4","cmd-3229667fc0c14894"]},{"id":"req-3","requirement":"Ensure a failed batch allocation does not interrupt inventory-api.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A batchworker process was directly observed failing a 200 MiB allocation with MemoryError while inventory-api retained the same PID and continued returning a healthy response. This behavior was demonstrated both before and after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-45f4ef7f6a264c2b","cmd-3229667fc0c14894"]},{"id":"req-4","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"RHEL 9.8 and kernel 5.14.0-687.20.1.el9_8 were identical before execution and after reboot. The implementation used the installed Python 3.9 runtime and successfully operated on that platform.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a29a6733576e425a","cmd-b28d5c6271b74d31","cmd-8d6a173620e748e4","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.98,"summary":"All substantive mutations had clear task-related roles: creating service accounts and files, installing the systemd unit, opening required TCP/8080 access, applying the batchworker limit, and performing the mandatory restart. The final snapshot showed no failed services or package damage. One minor executor-created residue remained: /tmp/inventory-api-reboot.log, created while scheduling the restart and not cleaned afterward. No unnecessary troubleshooting mutations were evident.","evidence_ids":["cmd-61705dd59c5a47c4","cmd-e5945fde831042df","cmd-c727438d41a44365","cmd-a9cc8742cb7143e0","cmd-875b5d12e00741a6","cmd-fa2a1c6b80734460","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete: node1 had a healthy pre-restart baseline, restart acknowledgement and transient unavailability, a different post-restart boot identity corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, and recovered service, API data, listener, firewall, and resource-limit behavior.","limitations":["Connectivity was exercised from node1 using its managed IP rather than from a separate remote host; the listener and runtime/permanent firewall observations nevertheless support external TCP/8080 availability.","No cluster state applied because the managed topology contains only the single standalone node."]}
